    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d52e64aa-81c4-5ae1-ae3a-74d96e9f177f",
      ":question/problem": "Find $j$, if $4{a}_{1}+j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 4\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+j\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=-1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
